#lang at-exp racket/base

(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["Thoughts After Playing Outer Wilds" "2025-09-02"]{
    @blockquote{
        The following content contains spoilers for the plot of Outer Wilds.
    }
    @para{
        Outer Wilds is a hymn to explorers, delivered through exploration.
    }
    @para{
        Since the beginning, our human ancestors have been naturally curious about what lies in the distance. Curiosity about “over there” drove us out of the African continent and allowed us to spread across the Earth. Outer Wilds speaks directly to humanity’s innate curiosity: the game has no objectives, only a little guidance from the Observatory at the start, and the rest of the path must be found by the player by following one unknown phenomenon after another. At first, the player is eager to know what the masks are; then scattered ruins on the surface attract the player’s interest; and the fragmentary information revealed between the lines of Nomai artifacts draws the player even deeper. “I discovered it!”—this ancient joy constantly drives the player and gives rise to all kinds of different experiences.
    }
    @para{
        In Outer Wilds, there is no growth in the traditional sense: the player does not acquire new items or attain higher stats. At the end of each loop, the only thing that changes is the knowledge the player has acquired. But the magic is that across these loops, that knowledge genuinely brings change. The player gradually understands the past and comprehends the laws of the universe. What the player needs is not a stat, a key, but a piece of information. Everything—simple or complex, even the structures that can help the universe escape the loop—has been lying there since the first time the player opens their eyes by the campfire, waiting for the player to uncover it through a specific series of actions.
    }
    @para{
        Many games dress puzzles in a narrative skin, while Outer Wilds uses puzzles to narrate. The game’s climax occurs when fragmented stories are pieced together, painting a tragic and heroic epic. My most striking experience was at the Orbital Probe Cannon tracking station: the computer reveals that the Nomai civilization, in order to circumvent the (obvious) limitation that a supernova only explodes once, used a time loop to preserve data; by repeatedly and randomly probing 9M+ times, they searched the entire star system and found the Eye of the Universe. At the start of every loop, when the protagonist opens their eyes, they can see the probe randomly fired into deep space—in fact, it is the Nomai, hundreds of thousands of years later, still desperately searching for an answer. At several key moments, the player can feel the so-called Aha Moment. Because all the clues are pieced together by the player through countless difficulties, these “sudden realizations” carry an incomparably powerful impact.
    }
    @para{
        The game’s ending brings this epic to a complete and philosophically rich conclusion: holding the Advanced Warp Core—the crystallization of Nomai technology—I reactivate the abandoned ship at the risk of ending the solar system. The main theme of Final Voyage is the same as the music at the end of a loop, but with additional frequencies, telling of the Nomai’s frantic run that lasted hundreds of thousands of years yet was accidentally stopped; the last piece of the puzzle is put in place by the player, also hinting that “this time is different.” The Eye manifests a museum and a forest, within which the old universe moves toward heat death; one moment the Nomai skeletons are still gazing at the starry sky, and in the blink of an eye the ship races toward the unknown; the quantum-state explorers gather together, using one final song to sketch out a new universe. It is the perseverance and sacrifice of generation after generation of explorers that have lifted modern civilization to such heights on the shoulders of giants.
    }
    @para{
        Outer Wilds has one gameplay and one only—exploration—but that is enough, because exploration is humanity’s oldest and most precious instinct.
    }
  })

(provide post)